<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Łęczyca Voivodeship</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Łęczyca Voivodeship was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, formed from the former Duchy of Łęczyca after its incorporation into the crown in the 14th century. Its seat was the town of Łęczyca in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It existed until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793, when its territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leczyca-voivodeship.jpg" alt="Łęczyca Voivodeship" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragment-historycznej-choragwi-Ziemi-Leczyckiej-XVI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The voivodeship covered 4,080 square kilometres of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, from Kłodawa in the north to the Pilica river in the south, where it bordered Sandomierz Voivodeship; its other neighbours were the voivodeships of Sieradz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a>, Brześć Kujawski and Rawa. It belonged to the Greater Poland Province and was divided into three counties, Łęczyca, Brzeziny and Orłów, of which Łęczyca County covered more than half the area.</p><p>In the 16th century it had 74 Roman Catholic parishes, 25 towns and 875 villages, and it was the second most densely populated voivodeship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> after Brześć Kujawski. Its towns included Zgierz, Kłodawa, Stryków, Brzeziny, Inowłódz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodz">Łódź</a>, which was a small town until the 19th century and for centuries belonged to the voivodeship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Land of Łęczyca became a separate duchy, ruled by princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>, after the 1138 Testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a>. King Ladislaus the Short reunited it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and made it a voivodeship. Because the local people were used to their own legal system, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> kept a separate official there, and in 1418 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> of Łęczyca drew up rules of their own, the 27 articles of the Constitutiones Terrae Lanciciensis generales.</p><p>The voivodeship had five senators in the Senate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, from 1569 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>: the Voivode of Łęczyca, the Castellan of Łęczyca and the castellans of Brzeziny, Inowłódz and Konary. The local nobility met at sejmiks in Łęczyca, where they elected four deputies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and two to the Greater Poland Tribunal at Piotrków Trybunalski. Chief starostas resided at Łęczyca, Inowłódz, Zgierz and Kłodawa. Among its voivodes were Stanisław Radziejowski, from 1627 to 1637, and Stefan Gembicki, from 1639 to 1653.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Łęczyca Voivodeship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When he died in 1352 it was incorporated into the Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385) as a province, later the <b>Leczyca Voivodeship</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-leczyca">Duchy of Leczyca</a></li></ul></section>
